<h2>Reasons: the heart of the modern award</h2>
<p>The 1996 Act reversed the old position in which unreasoned awards were the norm. The discipline of Section 31(3) is what allows the limited judicial review the Act contemplates to function at all: a court asked to test an award against public policy or patent illegality can only do so against the tribunal's stated reasoning. In practice, the quality bar is functional rather than literary — the award must let a reader understand what was decided and why, linking the evidence to the conclusions on each claim. Tribunals protect their awards by dealing with each head of claim and defence distinctly; parties protect themselves by framing claims in a structure the award can follow, since a tribunal's omission to decide a claim that was clearly put is itself a recognised ground of attack, and the Act's correction machinery — Section 33's additional-award and interpretation procedure, and the court's power under Section 34(4) to remit so the tribunal can eliminate curable defects — exists precisely for reasoning gaps short of invalidity.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>The consent-award exception deserves care.</strong> An award on agreed terms needs no reasons, but it must still comply with the rest of Section 31 — writing, signatures, date, place, delivery — and it has the same status and effect as any other award on the merits. Parties settling mid-arbitration should insist the settlement is embodied in a Section 30 award rather than a bare agreement: the award is enforceable as a decree; the agreement is only a contract.</p>
</div>

<h2>Interest and costs: the money the formalities carry</h2>
<p>Section 31(7) splits the timeline. For the period between cause of action and award, the tribunal has discretion over interest — rate, rests and the sums it attaches to — but the contract governs where it speaks; a contractual bar or fixed rate binds the tribunal. From the award to payment, the award controls, and where it is silent the statutory default rate applies — two percentage points above the prevailing current repo-linked rate the provision prescribes. The practical drafting lesson runs to both instruments: contracts should state their interest regime deliberately, and tribunals should award interest expressly for both periods, because silence breeds execution-stage litigation that outlives the arbitration.</p>

<h2>Delivery: the step parties forget to prove</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Tribunal side.</strong> Deliver signed copies — not photocopies of a signed original retained elsewhere — to each party, and record the dispatch and receipt.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Award-holder side.</strong> Confirm the opponent's receipt date in writing; the Section 34 window you must wait out, and the enforcement date you may act on, both depend on it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Award-debtor side.</strong> Diarise three months from receipt — the Section 34(3) period, extendable by a maximum of thirty further days on sufficient cause and no more. The delivery date is the single most unforgiving date in Indian arbitration.</div>
</div>

<div class="check">
  <p>Before filing or resisting any post-award proceeding, audit the award against Section 31: signatures and explanations, reasons or a valid dispensation, date and place, interest structure, costs, and proof of delivery. The audit takes an hour and decides which arguments exist.</p>
</div>

